    "ts": "1731113580.000000",
    "client_msg_id": "dcf8cb995da919dbdc",
    "text": "Thanks niaj! One question \u2014 is the timestamp field called `recorded_at` or `event_time`? I saw `event_time` mentioned in an old RFC doc.",
    "team": "T0EXACME01",
    "user_profile": {
      "name": "carol",
      "real_name": "Carol",
      "display_name": "carol",
      "team": "T0EXACME01",
      "is_restricted": false,
      "is_ultra_restricted": false
    }
  },
  {
    "user": "U6525ABCCE7",
    "type": "message",
    "ts": "1731121260.000000",
    "client_msg_id": "6f09bd239d91ec0c73",
    "text": "It's `recorded_at`. The old RFC used `event_time` as a placeholder \u2014 disregard that. Final spec uses `recorded_at` as an **integer milliseconds** Unix timestamp.",
    "team": "T0EXACME01",
    "user_profile": {
      "name": "niaj",
      "real_name": "Niaj",
      "display_name": "niaj",
      "team": "T0EXACME01",
      "is_restricted": false,
      "is_ultra_restricted": false
    }
  },
  {
    "user": "U48181ACD22",
    "type": "message",
    "ts": "1731133440.000000",
    "client_msg_id": "626efbf66998cce299",
    "text": "Getting started on the migration. My initial read: v2 uses `event_name`, `author_id`, `attributes`, `recorded_at`. Does that look right?",
    "team": "T0EXACME01",
    "user_profile": {
      "name": "bob",
      "real_name": "Bob",
      "display_name": "bob",
      "team": "T0EXACME01",
      "is_restricted": false,
      "is_ultra_restricted": false
    }
  },
  {
    "user": "U522B276A35",
    "type": "message",
    "ts": "1731143160.000000",
    "client_msg_id": "4e63ce885fb6cbac77",
    "text": "Looks close but I think the user field might be different \u2014 niaj can confirm?",
    "team": "T0EXACME01",
    "user_profile": {
      "name": "alice",
      "real_name": "Alice",
      "display_name": "alice",
